    "field": "Earth system science",
    "problem": "Current estimates of atmospheric soluble iron underestimate the contribution from anthropogenic combustion, a complex climate forcer, because they are based on limited observational data that do not account for recently measured high concentrations of light-absorbing magnetite from fossil fuels.",
    "method": "The authors incorporate recent measurements of anthropogenic magnetite into a global aerosol model to re-evaluate the atmospheric burden, direct radiative forcing, and soluble iron deposition, demonstrating that combustion iron is a more significant and complex climate forcer than previously understood.",
    "task": "The task is to reassess the climate impact of anthropogenic combustion iron by incorporating recent magnetite measurements into a global aerosol model to quantify its atmospheric burden, deposition flux to the ocean, and direct radiative forcing.",
